e ball or making any errors in these frames.  \n- The frames show Venus in close-up (frames 3–6, 24–26) looking focused or tired, but **no gameplay action** where she hits the ball or commits an error.  \n- Frame 27–30 show Serena serving, but Venus is not shown returning or missing the ball.\n\n❌ **No evidence of two unforced errors by Venus.**\n\n---\n\n### **3. Serena Williams serving aces**\n\n- Frame 27–30 show Serena serving (from the baseline, tossing the ball up).  \n- However, **we do not see the ball land in or out**, nor do we see Venus’s reaction to the serve.  \n- **No confirmation of an ace** — we don’t see the ball land untouched in the service box or Venus failing to return it.  \n- The score remains 4-5 — **no point is awarded to Serena in these frames**, so even if she served, the point was not completed.\n\n❌ **No confirmed aces.**\n\n---\n\n### **4. Serena closing in on the set with three set points**\n\n- The scoreboard shows **Serena leading 5-4**, which means she is one game away from winning the set — **not three set points**.  \n- “Set points” refer to points within a game (e.g., 40-0, 40-15, 40-30), not games in the set.  \n- The score **4-5** means Serena needs to win **one more game** to win the set — not three points.  \n- The point score shown is **30-30** (in frames 1–30), meaning **no set point has been reached yet**.\n\n❌ **Misrepresentation of score context — “three set points” is incorrect.**\n\n---\n\n### **5. Sequence of events matching answer choice D**\n\n- Answer D describes a **sequence**:  \n  1. Two unforced errors from Venus  \n  2. Serena closes in on the set  \n  3. Another ace brings three set points  \n  4. Serena hits an ace  \n\n- **None of these events are visually confirmed** in the provided frames.  \n- The **score does not advance** to 4-6.  \n- **No point is completed** in the sequence shown.  \n- The frames are **pre-serve or mid-serve**, not post-point.\n\n❌ **The sequence described in answer D does not occur 